rgest in your dataset).\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"change\": \"Make the backup workload long enough to cover the probe window.\",\n        \"how\": \"Instead of a single CTAS, do repeated full-table scans (read-only) for N seconds, or chunked copy by key ranges with sleeps removed so it stays busy.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"change\": \"Use a backup-like pattern that forces sustained reads without finishing quickly.\",\n        \"how\": [\n          \"Run multiple concurrent read streams: e.g., 2-4 sessions each doing `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM order_line` or `SELECT SUM(...)` (full scan) repeatedly for 60s.\",\n          \"If permitted, use `SELECT ... INTO OUTFILE` to force disk writes while scanning (often closer to dump behavior) and ensure cleanup.\"\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"change\": \"Increase parallelism safely within constraints.\",\n        \"how\": \"Run 2-4 parallel backup queries (cap by `max_connection_usage_ratio`), and stop if CPU>90% or IO saturates beyond acceptable thresholds.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"anomaly_design_fixes\": [\n      {\n        \"change\": \"Avoid CTAS if your goal is 'backup' interference rather than data duplication.\",\n        \"why\": \"CTAS can be optimized and also creates/locks metadata; it may not sustain pure read IO contention long enough.\",\n        \"alternative\": \"Use read-heavy scans that mimic backup reads; or export-like operations if allowed.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"change\": \"If you keep CTAS, make it heavier and more realistic.\",\n        \"how\": [\n          \"Copy `order_line` and add secondary indexes after creation (index builds are heavy and long-running).\",\n          \"Copy multiple large tables sequentially without pauses so the job spans the probe duration.\"\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"suggested_revised_plan_example\": {\n    \"goal\": \"Cause sustained IO contention consistent with a table backup while measuring increased p95 latency and reduced QPS\",\n